# GitHub Auto Push Rules

## Trigger
When the user says any of the following (or close variations):
- "push to github"
- "push it"
- "commit and push"
- "sync to github"
- "push the code"

## Automatic Workflow
Execute these steps in order without asking for confirmation:

```bash
# 1. Stage all changes
git add -A

# 2. Generate a commit message based on changed files
# Format: "[MediaMitigator] <type>: <summary of changes>"
# Types: feat | fix | refactor | style | docs | test | chore
# Example: "[MediaMitigator] feat: add folder tree widget and drive selection step"

# 3. Commit
git commit -m "<generated message>"

# 4. Push to current branch
git push origin <current-branch>

# 5. Report back to user:
# - Branch pushed to
# - Number of files changed
# - Commit hash
# - Commit message used
```

## Commit Message Generation Rules
- Scan git diff --staged to understand what changed
- If new files added: "feat: add <module/feature names>"
- If existing files modified: "refactor/fix: <what changed>"
- If only docs/comments: "docs: update documentation"
- If requirements.txt changed: "chore: update dependencies"
- Keep message under 72 characters for the subject line
- If changes are large/mixed, use: "chore: update <N> files across <modules>"

## Branch Safety
- Never push directly to main/master without confirmation from user
- If on main/master, warn the user and ask which branch to push to
- All feature work should be on a branch named: feature/<short-description>

## Pre-Push Checklist (run silently, report failures)
- Check for any .env files accidentally staged → remove and warn
- Check for hardcoded API keys or passwords → warn if found
- Check that requirements.txt is up to date
